C = [
("h1", "2. Hosea: The Wife and the Betrothal"),
("p", "Claim: the first three chapters of Hosea tell the whole story of the wife in short compass. She is unfaithful, the husband pleads and declares the separation, he draws her to himself in the wilderness, he promises to betroth her for ever, and he names a long interval before she returns. The wife is the nation and the husband is the LORD, and the restoration is promised to “the children of Israel” in “the latter days.” No other people is named in the story."),
("sub", "2.1 The sign and its interpretation"),
("p", "The book opens with a command that is also a sign: “Go, take unto thee a wife of whoredoms and children of whoredoms: for the land hath committed great whoredom, departing from the LORD” (Hosea 1:2). The prophet’s household is the message. The reason is given in the same sentence, and the land is the one that has departed. In chapter 3 the sign is explained in terms that leave no doubt about who is meant: “Go yet, love a woman beloved of her friend, yet an adulteress, according to the love of the LORD toward the children of Israel, who look to other gods” (Hosea 3:1). The love of the prophet for an adulteress is set beside the love of the LORD for the children of Israel. The comparison is made by the text, and it is not left for the reader to make."),
("sub", "2.2 “She is not my wife”"),
("p", "The second chapter is a lawsuit within a household. The children are told to plead with their mother, because the husband has spoken the formula of separation: “she is not my wife, neither am I her husband” (Hosea 2:2). The charge is whoredom and adultery, and the mother is pleaded with to put them away. Nothing in the chapter suggests that the marriage was never made. The words presuppose it. A woman cannot cease to be a wife unless she was one, and Isaiah and Jeremiah, as the next chapters show, speak of the same separation as a putting away with a bill of divorce."),
("sub", "2.3 The wilderness and the door of hope"),
("p", "Then the chapter turns on a single word: “Therefore, behold, I will allure her, and bring her into the wilderness, and speak comfortably unto her” (Hosea 2:14). The husband is the one who acts. He leads her to the place of the espousals, the wilderness, and what he gives her there is a vineyard, a door of hope in the valley of Achor, and a song “as in the days of her youth, and as in the day when she came up out of the land of Egypt” (Hosea 2:15). The restoration is described in the language of the exodus because it is a return to the beginning of the marriage."),
("sub", "2.4 The betrothal for ever"),
("p", "The promise is then made in the first person and three times over: “I will betroth thee unto me for ever; yea, I will betroth thee unto me in righteousness, and in judgment, and in lovingkindness, and in mercies” (Hosea 2:19), and again, “I will even betroth thee unto me in faithfulness: and thou shalt know the LORD” (Hosea 2:20). The verbs are future. The betrothal is a thing the LORD will do “at that day” (Hosea 2:16), and its marks are the marks of his own character. The wife’s knowledge of the LORD is the last thing the promise names."),
("sub", "2.5 Many days, and then the latter days"),
("p", "The third chapter fixes the time scale. The woman is told that she is to wait: “Thou shalt abide for me many days; thou shalt not play the harlot, and thou shalt not be for another man: so will I also be for thee” (Hosea 3:3). The prophet then says what the waiting is. The children of Israel “shall abide many days without a king, and without a prince, and without a sacrifice, and without an image, and without an ephod, and without teraphim” (Hosea 3:4). These are the national marks of Israel, and their absence describes a nation without its king and its worship. The return follows: “Afterward shall the children of Israel return, and seek the LORD their God, and David their king; and shall fear the LORD and his goodness in the latter days” (Hosea 3:5)."),
("p", "The wife who waits and the nation that waits are one. The restoration is to a people who seek “David their king,” and the time of it is the latter days. A reader who takes the wife to be another company must say where the text turns from the children of Israel to someone else, and in Hosea 1 to 3 it does not turn."),
("tbl", "The table follows the story clause by clause.", [
    ("Hos 1:2", "“take unto thee a wife of whoredoms”", "The sign is a marriage, and the land is the wife"),
    ("Hos 2:2", "“she is not my wife, neither am I her husband”", "The separation is declared by the husband"),
    ("Hos 2:14", "“I will allure her, and bring her into the wilderness”", "The husband leads her to the place of the espousals"),
    ("Hos 2:15", "“as in the day when she came up out of the land of Egypt”", "The restoration is told in the language of the exodus"),
    ("Hos 2:19", "“I will betroth thee unto me for ever”", "The betrothal is for ever, and it is promised"),
    ("Hos 2:20", "“I will even betroth thee unto me in faithfulness”", "The ground is the husband’s faithfulness"),
    ("Hos 3:1", "“according to the love of the LORD toward the children of Israel”", "The sign is interpreted: the wife is Israel"),
    ("Hos 3:5", "“Afterward shall the children of Israel return”", "The return follows the many days, in the latter days"),
  ], [0.14, 0.50, 0.36]),
("sub", "2.6 The names reversed"),
("p", "The children of the marriage carry names that tell the story. A daughter is named for the mercy that will no more be had “upon the house of Israel” (Hosea 1:6), and a son is named for the word “ye are not my people” (Hosea 1:9). Hosea gives each name its reversal: “I will have mercy upon her that had not obtained mercy; and I will say to them which were not my people, Thou art my people” (Hosea 2:23). That verse is quoted by Paul in Romans 9, and the use he makes of it is taken up in Chapter 13. Here it is enough to note that in Hosea itself the reversal is the reversal of Israel’s condition and that the verse stands in the same chapter as the betrothal."),
("sub", "2.7 What this chapter does not say"),
("bul", [
    "It does not say that Hosea’s own marriage is a pattern for every marriage. Its subject is the LORD and Israel.",
    "It does not say that the wife is the church. No sentence in Hosea 1 to 3 speaks of any company but the children of Israel.",
    "It does not give a date for the betrothal beyond “that day” and “the latter days.” The chapter fixes the order of events and leaves the calendar to other passages.",
  ]),
("call", "Where the argument stands", "In Hosea the wife is the nation, the husband is the LORD, the separation is real, and the betrothal for ever is promised to the children of Israel after many days, in the latter days, when they seek the LORD and David their king."),
]
